  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Kissi\"\nsubtitle: \"Guinea\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Kissi are a West African ethnolinguistic group. |\n| Where | Guinea, Liberia and Sierra Leone. |\n| How many | They are Guinea’s fourth largest ethnic group and make up 6.2% of its population. |\n| Language | Kissi, a Mel language in the Niger–Congo family. |\n| Religion | Many Kissi are Christian, while most continue traditional religion with ancestor worship and prayer to deceased relatives. |\n| Known for | Basket-making · Vertical-loom weaving · Ironworking · Kissi pennies · Bundu or Bandu society objects |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Kissi live in Guinea, Liberia and Sierra Leone. Their material culture includes weaving, basket-making, iron tools, pottery, musical instruments, masks and society objects. Kissi women spin yarn for men’s looms. Traditional religion includes ancestor worship and prayer to deceased relatives.\n\n## Material culture\n\nKissi material culture is known for woven goods, plant fibres, ironwork, pottery, instruments and objects linked to Bundu or Bandu societies.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nKissi weaving uses vertical looms, plant fibres and hand-spun cotton.\n\n- **Vertical loom** — Kissi weavers are well known for using vertical looms.\n- **Weaving implement** (*kpaba*) — The kpaba has a wooden frame with parallel heddles, while its other sides and heddles are made of rotang.\n- **Cotton holder** (*temuo*) — The temuo is a round wooden stick that branches into four parts and holds cotton.\n- **Spindle** (*kbendeleng*) — The kbendeleng is a spindle with a long heavy shaft and a conical soapstone weight called dindo.\n- **Head covering** (*Fujó*) — The Fujó is made from pisang or Urèna fibre and decorated with cauris.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nThe documented clothing is linked mainly to Bundu or Bandu societies, ceremonies and hunting.\n\n- **Dance skirt** (*Jo*) — The Jo is a plant-fibre dance skirt worn over imported cotton cloth.\n- **Net shirt** (*Kondoyila*) — The Kondoyila is a grey cotton net shirt with large knotted meshes and cauris around the neck and sleeves.\n- **Hunter’s tunic** — A hunter’s or warrior’s tunic was made from hand-spun and hand-woven cotton strips, with cotton-wrapped amulets and small horns sewn to the back of the neck.\n- **Girl’s head covering** (*Fujó*) — After a Bundu ceremony, an older woman braided the Fujó into a girl’s hair, and it was later removed.\n\n### Architecture\n\nThe sources do not describe Kissi buildings or settlements.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nKissi smiths made iron tools and weapons, while households used pottery, baskets and farm objects.\n\n- **Palm-fruit knife** (*Kissi penny*) — One knife was forged from a Kissi penny and was mainly used to harvest palm fruits.\n- **Fighting dagger** — A curved iron dagger from Liberia was used in fighting.\n- **Black earthen vessel** — Pottery records include a round black earthen vessel with an incised and fired pattern.\n- **Palm-oil sieve** — A sieve was used to produce palm oil.\n- **Bird sling** (*fanda*) — The fanda is a sling used from rice-field platforms to drive rice birds away.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nCauris decorate Bundu or Bandu clothing and head coverings.\n\n- **Cauris on a head covering** (*Fujó*) — Cauris decorate the Fujó head covering worn by girls after Bundu or Bandu ceremonies.\n- **Cauris on a net shirt** (*Kondoyila*) — Cauris decorate the neck opening and sleeves of the Kondoyila net shirt.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nKissi instruments include drums, rattles, a lamellophone, a scraper and an ivory horn.\n\n- **Goblet drum** (*sangbaa*) — The sangbaa is a goblet-shaped drum with a skin, a wooden stick, tension cords and a tubular foot ending in a tripod.\n- **Seven-tongue instrument** (*sansa*) — The sansa has seven metal tongues attached to a wooden board with a plaited design.\n- **Seed rattle** (*tjekó*) — The tjekó is a rattan-vine rattle with a calabash base filled with Canna indica seeds.\n- **Calabash rattle** (*seláng*) — The seláng is a calabash rattle with an attached bead rattle and belongs to an ensemble including the sangbáa.\n- **Grooved scraper** — A scraper made from Raphia vinifera has fifty-one grooves that are rubbed with a stick or palm-nut in time with dance or song.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nDance objects are especially connected with Bundu or Bandu society events.\n\n- **Ankle rattle** (*Jajei*) — The Jajei is an ankle rattle made for girls of the Bundu society.\n- **Women’s dance rattle** — Women used a hollow bottle-like fruit covered with seed beads at Bundu parties when a masked Bundu “devil” appeared.\n- **Horned mask** — A wooden mask is recorded with two horns, four probable chimpanzee teeth and animal fur.\n- **Dance skirt** (*Jo*) — The Jo formed part of a Bundu or Bandu dance costume.\n- **Net shirt** (*Kondoyila*) — The Kondoyila formed part of a Bundu or Bandu dance costume.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nThe sources describe Bundu or Bandu ceremonies for girls but no annual festival calendar.\n\n- **Bundu bush ceremony** (*Bundu*) — After returning from the Bundu bush, girls wore Fujó head coverings braided into their hair, which were later removed.\n- **Bandu society ceremony** (*Bandu*) — The Bandu society is linked to the Fujó, Jajei, Jo and Kondoyila.\n- **Circumcision razor** — A razor is recorded as an instrument for circumcision, but the sources give no wider details about the rite.\n\n## Foodways\n\nThe documented food-related objects concern rice fields, palm oil and palm fruits.\n\n- **Rice-field sling** (*fanda*) — The fanda was used to drive rice birds away from platforms in rice fields.\n- **Palm-oil sieve** — A sieve was used for producing palm oil.\n- **Palm-fruit knife** (*Kissi penny*) — A knife made from a Kissi penny was chiefly used for harvesting palm fruits.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nThe sources do not describe Kissi stories, proverbs, performers or literary traditions.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nKissi is a tonal Mel language, and traditional religion centers on ancestors and deceased relatives.\n\n- **Kissi language** (*Kisi, Kisiei*) — Kissi, also called Kisi or Kisiei, is a Mel language in the Niger–Congo family.\n- **Northern dialect** (*kisiduei*) — The northern dialect, kisiduei, is spoken in Guinea and Sierra Leone.\n- **Southern dialect** (*kpekeduei*) — The southern dialect, kpekeduei, is spoken in Liberia and Sierra Leone.\n- **Ancestor worship** — Ancestor worship and prayer to deceased relatives are common.\n- **Soapstone figures** — Carved soapstone figures and heads were made before colonial contact, but their purpose is uncertain.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Bundu* — a society for girls\n- *Bandu* — another name for Bundu\n- *kpaba* — weaving implement\n- *temuo* — wooden cotton holder\n- *kbendeleng* — spindle\n- *dindo* — conical soapstone spindle weight\n- *Kondoyila* — grey cotton net shirt\n- *Jo* — plant-fibre dance skirt\n- *Urèna* — plant fibre\n- *Fujó* — fibre head covering\n- *Jajei* — ankle rattle\n- *sangbaa* — goblet-shaped drum\n- *sangbáa* — name used for the drum in an ensemble\n- *sansa* — seven-tongue metal instrument\n- *tjekó* — seed rattle\n- *seláng* — calabash and bead rattle\n- *fanda* — sling for driving away birds\n- *kisiduei* — northern Kissi dialect\n- *kpekeduei* — southern Kissi dialect\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- George Tucker Childs, *A grammar of Kisi: a southern Atlantic language*, Mouton de Gruyter, 1995.\n- G. Tucker Childs, *A Dictionary of the Kisi Language. With an English-Kisi Index*.\n- Denise Paulme, *Les Gens du Riz: Les Kissi de Haute-Guinée*, Librairie Plon, 1954 and 1970.\n- [Kissi people — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kissi_people)\n- [Kissi language — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kissi_language)\n- Museum of World Culture and Museum of Ethnography catalogue records cited in the object catalogue supplied for this atlas.\n- British Museum catalogue records cited in the object catalogue supplied for this atlas.\n\n",
